<p>{% person "gene-helm" %}Gene{% endperson %}ral contractor {% person "n-a-freeburg" %}N. A. Freeburg{% endperson %}, who was of Swedish descent, built the house in 1907 for his bride, {% person "esther-freeburg" %}Esther{% endperson %}, intending it as their permanent home. Freeburg and his partner, {% person "charles-kidd" %}Charles Kidd{% endperson %}, constructed several prominent Mandan buildings, including the {% place "lyon-weigel-home" %}H. R. Lyon home at 309 Fourth Avenue NW{% endplace %} and the {% place "first-national-bank-building" %}First National Bank Building{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>The Freeburgs sold the house in 1910 to {% person "gustav-a-renden" %}Gustav A. Renden{% endperson %}, co-owner of the {% place "mandan-flour-mill" %}Mandan Roller Mill{% endplace %} and a founder of the {% place "mandan-electric-company" %}Mandan Electric Company{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>{% person "h-a-jones" %}H. A. Jones{% endperson %} purchased it in 1940, followed by bank examiner {% person "oren-baertsch" %}Oren Baertsch{% endperson %} in 1942 and {% place "piggly-wiggly" %}Piggly Wiggly{% endplace %} owner {% person "bill-joersz" %}Bill Joersz{% endperson %} in 1949.</p>

<p>Attorney {% person "maurice-lagrave" %}Maurice LaGrave{% endperson %} purchased the house in 1959. Gene and {% person "nola-helm" %}Nola Helm{% endperson %}, owners of {% place "helm-brothers-sand-and-gravel" %}Helm Brothers Sand and Gravel{% endplace %}, followed in 1967. Intending to make it their permanent home, the Helms undertook a major restoration. They added a room at the rear, a utility room, and a garage while retaining the original stairs, sliding doors, woodwork, pillars, full-wall bookshelves, windows, and distinctively finished stone foundation. The former carriage house at the rear of the property is now a separate residence next door.</p>

<p>{% person "chuck-esser" %}Chuck{% endperson %} and {% person "janet-esser" %}Janet Esser{% endperson %} of Camas, Washington, purchased the home in 1990. Janet's maternal and paternal ancestors had immigrated to the Sims area a century earlier.</p>

<p>The house has a warm, light atmosphere, and its front porch has long been a favorite gathering place for friends and family.</p>
